ppc-cpu: a seam for a port with no thread to spare

kpx_cpu's interpreter, once entered, does not come back until the
Macintosh stops. Every existing host is fine with that because every
existing host has a spare thread: SheepShaver's 60 Hz tick, its PRAM
writer and its input all live in one, beside the emulator.

A host that has none — a browser, a bare-metal board — has nowhere to
stand. Its timer is an interrupt handler, where blocking on a disk is not
allowed, and the emulation core never yields. So it cannot write the PRAM
when the PRAM changes, and it cannot hand input to the ADB code from the
thread that reads it.

This adds the seam, as a knob rather than a platform test:
PPC_CHECK_TICKS is the number of interpreted instructions between two
calls to powerpc_check_ticks(), which the port defines, and 0 — the
default — compiles the whole thing away. Both interpreter loops carry the
count, since which one runs depends on PPC_DECODE_CACHE.

The count itself is a variable, ppc_check_ticks_quantum, which starts at
PPC_CHECK_TICKS. A fixed count of instructions is a rate only on a machine
whose speed never varies, and an interpreter's varies with what the guest
is doing: measured on one board at 87 Hz averaged and 135 Hz instantaneous
over the same run. A port that wants a rate can therefore measure this
seam and write the quantum back, and one that never touches it keeps the
old fixed behaviour exactly.

mihaip/macemu (branch infinite-mac-kanjitalk755) carries the same seam for
WebAssembly, hardcoded to 50000 and to its own #ifdef; this is the same
idea named for what it does.

diff --git a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-config.hpp b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-config.hpp
index 7563fd02..891b5631 100644
--- a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-config.hpp
+++ b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-config.hpp
@@ -49,6 +49,26 @@
 #endif
 
 
+/**
+ *	PPC_CHECK_TICKS
+ *
+ *		Number of interpreted instructions between two calls to
+ *		powerpc_check_ticks(), which the port defines. 0 disables the
+ *		check altogether, which is what a host with a spare thread
+ *		wants: it has somewhere better to do periodic work.
+ *
+ *		A host without threads has nowhere else at all. Its timer runs
+ *		in an interrupt handler, where it may not block on a disk; the
+ *		interpreter, once entered, does not come back. This is the seam
+ *		that gives such a port a place to stand — inside the loop,
+ *		between two instructions, on the emulation thread.
+ **/
+
+#ifndef PPC_CHECK_TICKS
+#define PPC_CHECK_TICKS 0
+#endif
+
+
 /**
  *	PPC_DECODE_CACHE
  *
diff --git a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.cpp b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.cpp
index 39a31ecb..c57cc8e3 100644
--- a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.cpp
+++ b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.cpp
@@ -578,6 +578,9 @@ void powerpc_cpu::execute(uint32 entry)
 	pc() = entry;
 #if PPC_EXECUTE_DUMP_STATE
 	const bool dump_state = true;
+#endif
+#if PPC_CHECK_TICKS
+	uint32 check_ticks_counter = 0;
 #endif
 	execute_depth++;
 #if PPC_DECODE_CACHE || PPC_ENABLE_JIT
@@ -698,6 +701,13 @@ void powerpc_cpu::execute(uint32 entry)
 					} while (--n > 0);
 				}
 
+#if PPC_CHECK_TICKS
+				if (++check_ticks_counter >= ppc_check_ticks_quantum) {
+					check_ticks_counter = 0;
+					powerpc_check_ticks();
+				}
+#endif
+
 				if (!spcflags().empty()) {
 					if (!check_spcflags())
 						goto return_site;
@@ -740,6 +750,12 @@ void powerpc_cpu::execute(uint32 entry)
 #if PPC_EXECUTE_DUMP_STATE
 		if (dump_state)
 			dump_registers();
+#endif
+#if PPC_CHECK_TICKS
+		if (++check_ticks_counter >= ppc_check_ticks_quantum) {
+			check_ticks_counter = 0;
+			powerpc_check_ticks();
+		}
 #endif
 		if (!spcflags().empty() && !check_spcflags())
 			goto return_site;
diff --git a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.hpp b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.hpp
index f1739a5f..1cc598ff 100644
--- a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.hpp
+++ b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/ppc/ppc-cpu.hpp
@@ -515,4 +515,18 @@ inline void powerpc_cpu::trigger_interrupt()
 extern void HandleInterrupt(powerpc_registers *r);
 #endif
 
+#if PPC_CHECK_TICKS
+// Defined by the port. Called from the interpreter every
+// ppc_check_ticks_quantum instructions, on the emulation thread, with the CPU
+// between two instructions.
+extern void powerpc_check_ticks();
+
+// How many instructions between two of those calls. A count of instructions is
+// a rate only on a machine whose speed never varies, and an interpreter's does:
+// a port that wants a rate measures this seam and writes the quantum back. It
+// starts at PPC_CHECK_TICKS, so a port that never touches it keeps the old
+// fixed behaviour.
+extern uint32 ppc_check_ticks_quantum;
+#endif
+
 #endif /* PPC_CPU_H */
